# ADR-deviation marker — ADR-004 gate toggle amendment
- date: 2026-11-05
- branch: docs/adr-004-gate-toggle-amendment
- adr_governing: ADR-004 (publish pipeline safety contract)
- outcome: ADR-deviation
- lens-a-supporting-adr: saltzer-schroeder — fail-safe defaults. The ADR's
  own Environment-gate section accepts a single maintainer only because
  "layered mitigations replace the missing separation," and the approval
  click is one of those layers. An off switch that persists silently IS the
  undetected state the repo already sat in. The amendment is only safe if
  the off state is one-shot and self-restoring, not a mode.
- lens-b-supporting-deviation: alan-cooper — goal-directed design. The gate
  exists to carry the operator's intent, not to outlast it. A gate its owner
  cannot set aside gets routed around instead; this repo has the precedent,
  an --admin merge on PR #465 one session ago. An explicit, default-ON,
  logged toggle is more honest than ad-hoc bypass.
- decision-rationale: The operator asked for a default-approval gate with an
  override for nights they would rather trust the release and sleep. ADR-004
  blocks that outright ("No auto-publish. Every release requires operator
  approval at the Environment gate"), so the toggle needs the block amended
  rather than quietly crossed. Three facts make amending correct. The ADR
  files that block under "Blocks (until reconsidered)" and carries its own
  Revisit trigger, so reconsidering is the sanctioned path. The gate is
  empty today — protection_rules is [] — so default-ON-with-toggle is
  strictly stronger than what actually runs. And Saltzer's objection is
  answerable in the design: the override is one-shot inside a single
  /publish trust dispatch, which clears the reviewer and restores it after
  the publish, so no night ends with the gate open.
- new-adr-filed: none — amends ADR-004 in place per its own
  "until reconsidered" clause. Operator approved the amendment path on
  2026-11-05 after being shown both lenses.
